{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 6,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "olsson-eva",
  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B005",
        "laureates": [
          "Michel Della Negra",
          "Peter Jenni",
          "Tejinder Virdee"
        ],
        "citation": "For conceiving and building the ATLAS and CMS experiments, which independently established the Higgs boson."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B003",
    "B008"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain B005. The independent ATLAS and CMS observations established a major prediction of the Standard Model, and continued study makes the result exceptionally mature. From an experimental perspective, conceiving and building detectors capable of that discovery is a substantial achievement with a more specific basis for individual credit than claiming the discovery as the work of three people. The citation must keep the collaborations’ indispensable contributions visible.",
    "case_against": "Thousands of researchers designed, built, operated, and analyzed the experiments. The proposed names and their roles in both experiments require a careful historical assessment; leadership titles alone would not settle whether this particular trio merits the credit. The citation also must not imply that experimental leaders originated the Higgs mechanism or made the observations without their collaborations.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "You favor B010 for opening high-energy neutrino astronomy, while acknowledging that the Higgs case has clearer independent confirmation and that Halzen’s individual credit depends on evidence of decisive leadership.",
        "response": "The new observational window is a strong reason to consider IceCube, and I agree that its established flux matters even while sources and production mechanisms remain incompletely identified. For my choice, ATLAS and CMS provide an unusually direct, independent confirmation of a discrete discovery. Both cases face collaboration-scale attribution, but the historical question for B005 is whether these three made identifiable contributions to conceiving and building the experiments, not whether they alone made the discovery."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "You favor B005, emphasizing the mature independent observations but cautioning that the named leaders’ contributions must be distinguished from the collaborations’ work.",
        "response": "I agree that this is the decisive reservation. A citation to the conception and construction of ATLAS and CMS narrows the claim to an experimental achievement, but it does not by itself prove that the proposed trio captures the right individual contributions. Before a final award, the historical record should support each person’s role in the relevant experimental work, and the citation should not turn leadership into sole credit."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The specific contributions of Della Negra, Jenni, and Virdee to conceiving and building ATLAS and CMS need verification against the historical record.",
      "The boundary between recognizing experimental leadership and obscuring the indispensable work of the collaborations remains difficult."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
